        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "cd0576fd-cc51-512c-8c75-45da8c8ca27d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Alberta, Canada, United Kingdom\n- Systems: Constitution of Alberta, Westminster system\n- Concepts: unwritten constitution, laying before the house, parliamentary procedure\n- Roles: clerk (legislature)\n\nFacts:\n- The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\n- Alberta has an unwritten constitution, as is typical of Canadian provinces and Westminster systems.\n- The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modeled after the system that developed in the United Kingdom.\n- In parliamentary procedure, especially in the Westminster system, a document is laid before the house when it is formally recognized as available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.\n- A clerk in a legislature may keep custody of documents laid before the house, received, or produced, make records of proceedings, allocate office space, enroll members, and administer an oath of office.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "e4faf5a5-c207-58ac-a093-3b79d2389933",
        "text": "Page: Constitution of Alberta. Sentence: The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\nPage: Constitution of Alberta. Sentence: As is typical of all Canadian provinces, and Westminster systems more generally, Alberta's is an unwritten constitution.\nPage: Westminster system. Sentence: The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modelled after that which developed in the United Kingdom.\nPage: Laying before the house. Sentence: In parliamentary procedure, especially the Westminster system, a document is laid before the house or Laid on the Table of the House when it is formally recognised as having been made available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.\nPage: Clerk (legislature). Sentence: This may encompass keeping custody of documents lain before the house, received, or produced; making records of proceedings; allocating office space; enrolling of members, and administering an oath of office.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
